  "narrative": {
    "intro": "New York has received $518M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date. The federal buying picture in the state includes 26,041 awards with an average award size of $19.9K, and obligations fell 95.9% year over year. FY2027 to date is a partial fiscal-year total. Obligations peaked at $32B in FY22 and then declined through FY23 ($18.7B), FY24 ($17B), FY25 ($13.8B) and FY26 ($12.7B) before reaching $518M in FY27. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) is the largest industry by obligations at $154M. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer in FY2027 to date with $191M, followed by the Department of Energy with $144M. BROOKHAVEN SCIENCE ASSOCIATES LLC is the largest vendor in FY2027 to date with $154M. The totals here are contract obligations and current-year figures are fiscal year to date and therefore partial.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in New York changed over recent years?",
        "body": "Obligations fell from $32B in FY22 to $18.7B in FY23, $17B in FY24, $13.8B in FY25 and $12.7B in FY26. Obligations in FY27 to date are $518M, and obligations fell 95.9% year over year. FY27 to date is a partial fiscal-year total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and vendors in New York right now?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $191M and the Department of Energy obligated $144M in FY2027 to date. BROOKHAVEN SCIENCE ASSOCIATES LLC received $154M and WELCH ALLYN, INC. received $31.1M in FY2027 to date. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) accounted for $154M in obligations."
      }
    ]
